  "Question": "Given the larger of two circles with center \\(P\\) and radius \\(p\\) and the smaller with center \\(Q\\) and radius \\(q.\\) Draw \\(\\overline{PQ}.\\) Which of the following statements is false?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(p-q\\) can be equal to \\(\\overline{PQ}\\)",
    "B": "\\(p+q\\) can be equal to \\(\\overline{PQ}\\)",
    "C": "\\(p+q\\) can be less than \\(\\overline{PQ}\\)",
    "D": "\\(p-q\\) can be less than \\(\\overline{PQ}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Each statement says “can,” so we may use a different placement of the circles for each statement. Let \\(d=PQ\\). Since one circle is larger, \\(p>q>0\\).\n\nFor A, place the smaller circle inside the larger one so that they touch internally. Then \\(d=p-q\\).\n\nFor B, let the circles touch externally. Then \\(d=p+q\\).\n\nFor C, separate the circles by a gap. Then \\(d>p+q\\).\n\nFor D, external tangency also works: \\(d=p+q>p-q\\), because \\(q>0\\).\n\nThus A through D are all possible. None of these statements is false, so the answer is \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
